%I A398823 #8 Aug 12 2026 01:36:42
%S A398823 135,296,343,375,512,728,999,1160,1431,1592,1624,2240,2295,2375,2456,
%T A398823 2624,2727,3429,3591,3624,3752,3992,4023,4184,4671,4887,4913,5048,
%U A398823 5144,5319,5480,5696,5831,6183,6344,6375,6591,6655,6858,7479,7624,7640,7749,7911,8072,8127
%N A398823 Numbers k such that the powerful parts (A057521) of both k and k+1 are cubes that are larger than 1.
%C A398823 Numbers k such that k and k+1 are both terms in A398822.
%C A398823 The asymptotic density of this sequence is d = zeta(3) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2 + 1/p^3 - 2/p^4 + 2/p^5) - 2 * zeta(3) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2 - 1/p^4 + 2/p^5) + Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2) = 0.00446313020394845468759... . The number of terms not exceeding x is d * x + O(x^(2/3+eps)), where eps > 0 (Srichan et al., 2026, a consequence of Theorem 1.1).
%H A398823 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398823/b398823.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398823 Teerapat Srichan, Nithi Rungtanapirom, and Saeree Wananiyakul, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/ita/2026022">On the distribution of consecutive M-free integers</a>, RAIRO - Theoretical Informatics and Applications, Vol. 60 (2026), Article 22.
%e A398823 135 = 3^3 * 5 is a term since its powerful part, 27 = 3^3, is a cube that is larger than 1, and the powerful part of 135 + 1 = 136 = 2^3 * 17 is 8 = 2^3, which is also a cube that is larger than 1.
%t A398823 q[k_] := q[k] = Module[{e = FactorInteger[k][[;; , 2]]}, AnyTrue[e, # > 1 &] && AllTrue[e, # == 1 || Divisible[#, 3] &]]; Select[Range[8200], q[#] && q[#+1] &]
%o A398823 (PARI) is1(k) = if(k == 1, 0, my(e = factor(k)[, 2]); vecmax(e) > 1 && #select(x -> (x > 1 && x%3), e) == 0);
%o A398823 isok(k) = is1(k) && is1(k+1);
%Y A398823 Subsequence of A398822.
%Y A398823 Cf. A002117, A057521, A065474.
%K A398823 nonn,easy
%O A398823 1,1
